<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Visigoths</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Visigothic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Visigoths were a Gothic Germanic people who entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in the late fourth century. They destroyed a Roman army and killed Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> at Adrianople in 378 CE, and under Alaric they sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 410 CE. They later established a kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-conquest-of-hispania">Umayyad conquest of Hispania</a> in 711.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/visigoths.jpg" alt="Visigoths" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous (Visigothic)Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visigothic_-_Pair_of_Eagle_Fibula_-_Walters_54421,_54422_-_Group.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Visigoths emerged from the Gothic tribes, probably descended from the Thervingi, whose name may mean forest people. Gothic tradition, on which the account of their origins largely rests, places the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>' beginnings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, with a migration south-east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, though archaeology does not support that legend.</p><p>The name Visigoths was apparently coined by the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassiodorus">Cassiodorus</a> about 507, on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, from the older tribal name Vesi, which is Gothic for good. Until then they were called simply <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>. They spoke an eastern Germanic language that was distinct by the 4th century. They had been Christianized while still outside the empire, but adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> rather than the Nicene version followed by most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, who considered them heretics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 376 the Gothic leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a> asked the Eastern Roman Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> to let his people settle south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, seeking refuge from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>. Famine followed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> abused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, even forcing them to trade away their children, which led to revolt and six years of plundering in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople">Battle of Adrianople</a> in 378 the Roman army was slaughtered and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaric-i">Alaric I</a> led the Visigoths from 395. In 397 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadius">Arcadius</a> named him military commander of the eastern Illyrian prefecture, and the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 410 followed. In 418 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> settled the Visigoths in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Gallia Aquitania</a>, where a kingdom grew with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, and they later expanded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>.</p><p>Defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vouille">Battle of Vouillé</a> in 507 ended Visigothic rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, but the kingdom endured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Liuvigild</a> (568 to 586) failed to unite Arian and Catholic subjects, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reccared-i">Reccared I</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, a step confirmed at the Third Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 589. The kingdom collapsed in 711 after defeat by an Umayyad army at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalete">Guadalete</a>, where King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a> was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigothic-code">Visigothic Code</a>, set in writing in 654 under Chindasuinth, abolished separate laws for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, so that all subjects became hispani, and it protected the property rights of married women. Surviving churches include San Pedro de la Nave, and Reccopolis, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Liuvigild</a> to honour his son Reccared, is an archaeological site. The Treasure of Guarrazar, twenty-six votive crowns and gold crosses from the royal workshop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, shows the high point of Visigothic goldsmithery. After 711 the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> arose under Pelagius, marking the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Visigoths” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Hispania the Alans received Lusitania and Carthaginiensis in the division of 411, and their king Addac ruled the strongest of the invading groups until the <b>Visigoths</b> destroyed his army in 418.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 486 Clovis defeated Syagrius, ruler of the last Roman enclave in northern Gaul, at Soissons, and Syagrius was later handed over by the <b>Visigoths</b> and executed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…largest Roman province of Iberia, was governed from Tarraco for some five centuries, guarded the gold mines of the north-west with Rome's only Iberian legion, gave Latin literature Quintilian and Martial, and was the last part of Hispania to remain under Roman rule before the <b>Visigoths</b> took it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He claimed Honoria, sister of Valentinian III, as his bride with half the Western Roman Empire as dowry, and in 451 entered Gaul, where Romans and <b>Visigoths</b> fought him at the Catalaunian Plains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Alfonso III (866–910), the court sponsored historical writing, notably the Chronicle of Alfonso III and the Chronicle of Albelda, which presented the Asturian kings as heirs of the <b>Visigoths</b> destined to recover the whole of Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings stressed their descent from the <b>Visigoths</b>, and from the tenth century some styled themselves emperor (imperator), a claim to precedence over the other Christian rulers of Iberia: Alfonso VI called himself emperor of all Spain, and Alfonso VII was crowned emperor in León in 1135.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
